      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 295.46,
      "end_seconds": 301.89,
      "duration_seconds": 6.43,
      "text": "you are dehydrated and drowsy with a slow, odd rhythm on the monitor"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0037",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 305.65,
      "end_seconds": 317.08,
      "duration_seconds": 11.43,
      "text": "okay, that's enough for me. Okay, so my impression is this: It points towards a high calcium emergency. A kidney failure is also on my list"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0038",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 318.44,
      "end_seconds": 320.02,
      "duration_seconds": 1.58,
      "text": "Is this permanent?"
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0039",
      "speaker_id": "person_2",
      "role": "DOCTOR",
      "start_seconds": 322.09,
      "end_seconds": 351.3,
      "duration_seconds": 29.21,
      "text": "Nothing I've seen today points to an emergency. It looks like a high calcium emergency, but I'd like to confirm. I think urgent blood tests makes sense here, for confusion and polyuria. I'd rather check than guess, and this is the quickest way to know more. I'd like to arrange an ECG for hypercalcaemia. Let's use sodium chloride 0.9%, 1 litre, over one hour, single dose. That's for dehydration."
    },
    {
      "segment_id": "SEG0040",
      "speaker_id": "person_1",
      "role": "PATIENT",
      "start_seconds": 352.91,
      "end_seconds": 356.3,
      "duration_seconds": 3.39,
      "text": "Should I take that with food?"
    },
